#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Structural + pixel baseline of the shipped web UI, swept over every layout.

The whole web UI is one file — `crates/plurxd/src/web/index.html` — and the
layout arc rearranges it. There is no component test to lean on and no design
system to diff, so the only honest way to say "this refactor changed nothing"
is to photograph the app before and after and prove the photographs are
identical. That was G0's contract, and G3 found two holes in it (see
docs/UI-LAYOUTS-G3-DECISION.md §3 and §5/R1):

  * it captured and exited — the comparison was a `sha256sum` a human ran by
    hand, so nothing ever failed;
  * it photographed exactly ONE `(layout, theme, appearance)` triple, so a
    regression that only manifests under a non-default layout was invisible.
    Two real `plex` fixes were silently reverted and shipped as done, and this
    harness passed them: from `classic`'s point of view a plex-only fix is a
    correct no-op. Coverage shrank as the arc succeeded — 1 of 2 layouts then,
    1 of 5 by G6.

So this script now sweeps every registered layout and it compares.

    scripts/ui-baseline --self-host              # capture into --out
    scripts/ui-baseline --self-host --check      # capture, then FAIL on drift
    scripts/ui-baseline --self-host --update     # rewrite the committed golden
    scripts/ui-baseline --self-host --baseline target/ui-baseline-before
    scripts/ui-baseline --base-url http://nynuc:32400 --token "$PLURX_TOKEN"

`--self-host` boots a throwaway plurxd on a scratch data dir, seeds it with a
tiny generated library (ffmpeg, deterministic bytes, cached between runs),
rewrites every timestamp in its database to fixed constants, and drives that.
That is the mode CI and a pre-change/post-change comparison use. Pointing
`--base-url` at a real server captures the same routes against real content —
useful on Paul's Mac, but the results are then only comparable to other runs
against the same server with the same library.

---- the two tiers, and why there are two ---------------------------------

A PNG hash is host-dependent (Chromium build, rasteriser, font stack) and
path-dependent (Settings prints a library's paths verbatim, and the seeded
library lives under `--out`). A committed pixel manifest would therefore fail
on every machine that is not the one that wrote it, and the only way to keep
it green is to stop believing it. So the golden is split:

  PORTABLE TIER — committed, enforced everywhere including CI.
    `tests/ui-structure.golden` holds, per layout × route × viewport: the
    structural DOM digest (see DOM_JS), the tab-order dump, and the API
    request log. None of it contains a pixel, a path, a timestamp or a free
    string, so it is the same file on a Mac, in the sandbox and on a CI
    runner. This is the tier that catches what pixels miss and what the two
    lost fixes were: a class that stopped being emitted, a control that lost
    `aria-disabled`, a card that lost `tabindex`, a rail that renders but tags
    nothing.

  PIXEL TIER — never committed.
    `pixels.txt` in `--out` holds one sha256 per PNG. It is compared only
    against a previous LOCAL run, via `--baseline <dir>`, as a before/after
    check on a refactor by one person on one machine. Committing it would be
    committing a fact about a laptop.

---- output ---------------------------------------------------------------

All under `--out` (default `target/ui-baseline/`, covered by the `/target`
line in .gitignore):

    <layout>/<route>@<width>x<height>.png   one screenshot per capture
    structure.txt      the portable tier, exactly as `--update` would commit it
    pixels.txt         sha256 per PNG — the local-only tier
    focus.json         tab-order dump, kept because it reads better than the
    requests.json      flat golden when you are debugging one route

The gate fails (exit 1) if any route fails to render — a non-2xx document
response, an uncaught page error, a console error, or an empty `#main`/`#app` —
and, under `--check`, if the portable tier differs from the committed golden.

---- layouts and surfaces -------------------------------------------------

The layout list comes from `Object.keys(LAYOUTS)` evaluated IN THE PAGE, never
from parsing the HTML. The registry is the authority; a parsed copy drifts the
first time someone registers a layout in a shape the parser did not anticipate,
and it drifts silently, which is the exact failure mode R1 exists to end.

Each layout declares the `surfaces` it can run on, and `layoutId()` falls back
to `classic` on a surface a layout does not declare. So the sweep asks the page
what surface each viewport actually is (`surfaceClass()`), skips the pairs the
app would refuse, and — for the pairs it does capture — asserts that
`layoutId()` came back as the layout requested. Photographing a fallback under
the name of the thing that fell back is worse than not photographing it.

Determinism is the point. Everything the app derives from the wall clock, the
timezone, the locale, an animation, a font hint, a device pixel ratio or a
lazily-decoded image is pinned below, each with a comment saying which drift it
prevents. Two consecutive `--check` runs on an unchanged tree must both pass,
and so must a run at a different `--out` path; if they do not, the gate is
worthless and the difference is a bug in this file, not something to paper over
with a tolerance.

`--self-test-fail` deliberately breaks one route so the nonzero exit can be
demonstrated without hand-editing the app. It exists so the gate's own failure
path stays tested; a green run never touches it.

Known residuals — things the PIXEL tier is legitimately tied to, so compare
like with like and regenerate rather than argue with a diff. None of these
touch the portable tier; that is what makes the portable tier committable:

  * The checkout path. Settings prints a library's paths verbatim, and the
    seeded library lives under `<out>/media`. Two runs at different `--out`
    paths produce different Settings screenshots. (The portable tier records
    the presence of that text, never its content, and scrubs any absolute path
    that reaches a focus label — see `scrub()`.)
  * The host. Chromium's rasteriser and the bundled build both move with the
    image; PNG hashes do not travel between the sandbox and a Mac.
  * Two captures — `plex/item@1280x860` and `plex/library@1280x860` — still
    move between two runs of an unchanged tree in the sandbox, roughly one run
    in two. Measured, not assumed: 34 of 36 are stable. Both are plex desktop
    routes with rails, so the suspect is the same scroll/arrow interaction the
    `--rest-ms` wait tamed elsewhere. They are named here rather than chased
    because the pixel tier is a human-judged before/after aid and not a gate;
    if it ever becomes one, this is the bug to fix first. The portable tier is
    stable on all 36.
  * `--base-url`. A live server's own timestamps are not frozen (only the page
    clock is), so "3 min ago" on a real Activity page still ticks, and a real
    library changes the DOM digest too. That mode is for eyeballing a real
    library, not for a gate; `--self-host` is the mode that is provably
    deterministic, and `--check` refuses to run against anything else.
  * The player's stats overlay (`ⓘ Stats`) is never opened: it prints live
    timing in milliseconds and nothing would make it stand still.
"""

import argparse
import calendar
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import signal
import socket
from urllib.parse import urlparse
import sqlite3
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.request
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent

# The committed half of the golden. Lives under tests/ rather than target/
# precisely because it is portable: nothing in it is a pixel, a path or a
# clock, so the same bytes are correct on every machine and a drift in it is a
# reviewable line in a pull request rather than a rumour about a laptop.
GOLDEN = ROOT / "tests" / "ui-structure.golden"

# The instant the page believes it is. Everything relative the UI renders
# ("3d ago", "ends ~12:00 PM", a user's join date) is measured from here, and
# the seeded database is written *backwards* from it, so the two agree. A fixed
# absolute instant rather than "now minus something": "now" is what makes two
# runs differ in the first place.
FROZEN_EPOCH = calendar.timegm(time.strptime("2025-06-15T12:00:00", "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S"))

# Seeded-library constants. Every one of these is rendered somewhere (library
# names and paths in Settings, usernames in the header and Settings → Users),
# so they are named here rather than generated — a mkdtemp path would put a
# different string on screen every run.
SEED_USER = "baseline"
SEED_PASSWORD = "baseline-password"

# The fixture corpus. Tiny on purpose: nothing on screen cares how big the
# picture is, and CI pays for every encode. Durations differ per title so the
# runtime column and the resume arithmetic have something real to show.
#
# `codec` picks the encoder: "h264" is what a real library is full of, and
# "vp9" is the one pair (VP9 + Opus) the headless Chromium in the sandbox can
# actually decode — the player route needs a file that genuinely plays, so
# exactly one fixture is VP9.
FIXTURES = [
    ("movies", "Heat (1995)/Heat (1995).mp4", "h264", 12),
    ("movies", "Blade Runner (1982)/Blade Runner (1982).mp4", "h264", 9),
    ("more-movies", "The Signal (2021)/The Signal (2021).webm", "vp9", 6),
    ("more-movies", "Signal Fire (2003)/Signal Fire (2003).mp4", "h264", 7),
    ("shows", "Signal Hill (2019)/Season 01/Signal Hill - S01E01 - Cold Open.mp4", "h264", 5),
    ("shows", "Signal Hill (2019)/Season 01/Signal Hill - S01E02 - The Handoff.mp4", "h264", 4),
]

# Two shares in the *same* category, so Home's "movies · view all" resolves to
# the merged `#/category/movie` page rather than short-circuiting to the single
# library — the category route has to have a reason to exist.
LIBRARIES = [
    ("Movies", "movies", "movies"),
    ("More Movies", "movies", "more-movies"),
    ("TV Shows", "shows", "shows"),
]

# The two viewports, and whether the browser reports a touch pointer at each.
#
# `touch` is not decoration. `surfaceClass()` decides "mobile" from
# `(pointer: coarse)` AND width, not from width alone, so a 390px viewport with
# a mouse resolves to *desktop* — and a layout that declares no `mobile`
# surface would then be captured at 390x780 anyway, under a name the app never
# used. Emulating touch at the narrow viewport makes the surface the app
# resolves match the surface this table claims to be photographing, which is
# what makes the `surfaces` filter below mean anything at all.
VIEWPORTS = [
    {"width": 1280, "height": 860, "touch": False},
    {"width": 390, "height": 780, "touch": True},
]

# Animation, transition and caret are killed with a stylesheet rather than with
# a wait: a wait only makes the odds better. `caret-color` matters because the
# search box on the search route holds a caret that blinks at 500ms — a coin
# flip on every capture. `scroll-behavior` because the rails scroll smoothly,
# and a screenshot taken mid-glide is a different picture every time.
FREEZE_CSS = """
*, *::before, *::after {
  animation: none !important;
  animation-duration: 0s !important;
  animation-delay: 0s !important;
  transition: none !important;
  transition-duration: 0s !important;
  transition-delay: 0s !important;
  caret-color: transparent !important;
  scroll-behavior: auto !important;
}
"""

# Installed as an init script so it runs before the app's first line. Two jobs:
#
#  1. Freeze the clock. The app formats "just now", "3d ago", "ends ~12:00 PM"
#     and a user's join date straight off Date.now()/new Date(); without this
#     the same DOM renders differently one minute later. `new Date(x)` with an
#     argument is left alone — parsing an air date must still work.
#  2. Inject FREEZE_CSS at document-start, so no frame is ever painted with
#     transitions live. Adding it after load would leave the first paint (and
#     any capture racing it) animated.
#
# Math.random is *not* stubbed: the app uses it for playback/request ids that
# never reach the screen, and replacing it would be lying about the real app.
FREEZE_JS = """
(() => {
  const FROZEN = __FROZEN_MS__;
  const Real = Date;
  class Frozen extends Real {
    constructor(...args) { if (args.length === 0) super(FROZEN); else super(...args); }
    static now() { return FROZEN; }
  }
  Frozen.parse = Real.parse;
  Frozen.UTC = Real.UTC;
  window.Date = Frozen;

  // An init script runs before the parser has produced <html>, so there is
  // nothing to append to on the first call — hence the null guard and the two
  // later attempts. The earliest one that finds a root wins; the id makes the
  // rest no-ops.
  const style = () => {
    const root = document.head || document.documentElement;
    if (!root || document.getElementById("ui-baseline-freeze")) return;
    const el = document.createElement("style");
    el.id = "ui-baseline-freeze";
    el.textContent = __FREEZE_CSS__;
    root.appendChild(el);
  };
  style();
  document.addEventListener("readystatechange", style);
  document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", style);
})();
"""

# Descriptor for one focused element. Order of preference for the label is
# aria-label, then title, then the element's own trimmed text — the same order
# a screen reader resolves, so the dump reads like the thing being tested.
# Deliberately excludes `value`, `href` and anything else that carries an id or
# a typed string: a tab order must not change because a database handed out a
# different primary key.
#
# The label is NOT truncated here. It used to be cut to 60 characters in the
# page, and a label that contains a filesystem path would then have been cut at
# a different character for every `--out` — so the committed golden would carry
# the LENGTH of somebody's checkout path. Python scrubs first and truncates
# after (see `focus_label`); 400 is only a guard against a pathological label.
FOCUS_JS = """
() => {
  const el = document.activeElement;
  if (!el || el === document.body || el === document.documentElement) return null;
  const tag = el.tagName.toLowerCase();
  const id = el.id ? "#" + el.id : "";
  const cls = el.classList.length ? "." + Array.from(el.classList).join(".") : "";
  let label = el.getAttribute("aria-label") || el.getAttribute("title") || "";
  if (!label) label = (el.innerText || el.textContent || "");
  if (!label && el.placeholder) label = el.placeholder;
  label = label.replace(/\\s+/g, " ").trim().slice(0, 400);
  return tag + id + cls + (label ? ' "' + label + '"' : "");
}
"""

# The layout registry, read from the page rather than from the file.
#
# `LAYOUTS` is a top-level `const` in the <head> script, so it is a global
# lexical binding — visible to an evaluated function, absent from `window`.
# `plex` (and every layout after it) attaches its renderers to that same object
# from the body script, which is why this must run against a fully parsed
# document rather than against the HTML source. Parsing index.html for a layout
# list would produce a second, lagging copy of the authority; this cannot lag.
REGISTRY_JS = """
() => ({
  layouts: Object.keys(LAYOUTS).map(id => ({
    id,
    name: (LAYOUTS[id] && LAYOUTS[id].name) || id,
    surfaces: ((LAYOUTS[id] && LAYOUTS[id].surfaces) || []).slice().sort(),
  })),
  surface: surfaceClass(),
})
"""

# The structural DOM digest: what the page IS, with everything a pixel hash is
# tied to removed.
#
# This is the tier that catches the failures §3 of the G3 record describes and
# a screenshot cannot: a class that stopped being emitted, a control that lost
# `aria-disabled`, a card that lost `tabindex`, a rail that renders but tags
# nothing. Each of those is a semantic change behind pixels that either look
# fine or differ for a hundred unrelated reasons at once.
#
# Every element in the given roots becomes one line, `parent > self`, and equal
# lines are COUNTED rather than listed. Counting is what keeps the golden
# reviewable: six cards in a grid are one line reading `6`, so losing `unw` on
# all six shows up as one removed line and one added line instead of twelve.
# The single level of parent context is the cheapest structure worth keeping —
# it distinguishes a poster in a rail from a poster in a grid, so a card that
# moves between regions is not silently equivalent.
#
# What each descriptor carries, and what it deliberately does not:
#
#   in   tag, id, sorted class list, `role`, `tabindex`, the enumerated ARIA
#        states (below), `disabled`/`hidden`, and a bare `text` flag when the
#        element has non-whitespace text of its own.
#   out  free text content, `style`, `href`, `src`, `value`, `onclick`, and the
#        VALUE of any free-string ARIA attribute (`aria-label` on a card is a
#        title; `aria-valuenow` on the seek bar is a clock). Those are recorded
#        as `aria-label=*` — present or absent, never what they said.
#
# The exclusions are not squeamishness, they are the portability requirement:
# the Settings page prints a library's filesystem path, which is different at
# every `--out`, and a digest that carried text could never be committed.
# Classes are sorted because `classList` order is source order, and source
# order is a diff nobody should have to read.
DOM_JS = """
(roots) => {
  // ARIA attributes whose values are a closed set — a state, not a string.
  // These are the ones worth asserting on: `aria-disabled` going missing is
  // exactly the class of regression that ships looking correct.
  const ARIA_VAL = ["aria-disabled", "aria-current", "aria-expanded", "aria-selected",
                    "aria-checked", "aria-pressed", "aria-hidden", "aria-modal",
                    "aria-live", "aria-level", "aria-orientation", "aria-haspopup",
                    "aria-multiselectable", "aria-readonly", "aria-required",
                    "aria-invalid", "aria-busy", "aria-valuemin", "aria-valuemax"];
  const desc = (el) => {
    const tag = el.tagName.toLowerCase();
    let s = tag;
    if (el.id) s += "#" + el.id;
    // Array.from(): classList is a DOMTokenList, and SVG elements carry one
    // too (their `className` is an SVGAnimatedString and would stringify to
    // "[object SVGAnimatedString]").
    const cls = Array.from(el.classList).sort();
    if (cls.length) s += "." + cls.join(".");
    const bits = [];
    if (el.hasAttribute("role")) bits.push("role=" + el.getAttribute("role"));
    if (el.hasAttribute("tabindex")) bits.push("tabindex=" + el.getAttribute("tabindex"));
    for (const a of ARIA_VAL) if (el.hasAttribute(a)) bits.push(a + "=" + el.getAttribute(a));
    for (const n of el.getAttributeNames().filter(
           n => n.startsWith("aria-") && ARIA_VAL.indexOf(n) < 0).sort())
      bits.push(n + "=*");
    if (el.hasAttribute("disabled")) bits.push("disabled");
    if (el.hasAttribute("hidden")) bits.push("hidden");
    // `type` on a form control changes what the control IS, and it is a closed
    // set, so it belongs here in a way `value` never could. Only when the
    // attribute is actually written: `el.type` would report the DEFAULT for
    // every untyped button in the app, which is a fact about the HTML spec
    // rather than about this codebase, on several hundred lines of golden.
    if (el.hasAttribute("type")) bits.push("type=" + el.getAttribute("type"));
    // Own text only, not descendant text: otherwise every ancestor of a label
    // reports `text` and the flag stops distinguishing anything.
    let own = "";
    for (const n of el.childNodes) if (n.nodeType === 3) own += n.nodeValue;
    if (own.trim()) bits.push("text");
    if (bits.length) s += "[" + bits.join(" ") + "]";
    return s;
  };
  const out = {};
  const walk = (el, parent) => {
    const self = desc(el);
    const key = parent + " > " + self;
    out[key] = (out[key] || 0) + 1;
    for (const c of el.children) walk(c, self);
  };
  // "^" for a root's own line, so the roots are distinguishable from each
  // other and a root that failed to render at all is one obviously-missing
  // line rather than a silently smaller file.
  for (const sel of roots) { const el = document.querySelector(sel); if (el) walk(el, "^"); }
  return out;
}
"""


class Fail(Exception):
    """A route did not render. Collected, reported, and turned into exit 1."""


# ---- environment ----------------------------------------------------------


def find_browser(explicit):
    """Path to a headless Chromium, or None to let Playwright resolve its own.

    The sandbox ships browsers under PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH with a build
    number in the directory name (`chromium-1194`), and that number moves every
    time the image is rebuilt — hence a glob rather than the literal path.
    On a Mac there is a normal `playwright install` cache and Playwright finds
    it without help, so falling through to None is the right answer there.
    """
    if explicit:
        return explicit
    root = Path(os.environ.get("PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH", "/opt/pw-browsers"))
    if not root.is_dir():
        return None
    # Full `chrome` before `headless_shell`: the shell build is missing pieces
    # the player route needs (media decode among them), and a route that cannot
    # play is a route this gate cannot photograph.
    for pattern in ("chromium-*/chrome-linux/chrome", "chromium_headless_shell-*/chrome-linux/headless_shell"):
        found = sorted(root.glob(pattern))
        if found:
            return str(found[-1])
    return None


def free_port(preferred):
    """`preferred` if it is free, else whatever the kernel hands out.

    The port never reaches the screen, so a fallback cannot cost determinism —
    but a hard failure on a busy port would cost a run.
    """
    with socket.socket() as s:
        try:
            s.bind(("127.0.0.1", preferred))
            return preferred
        except OSError:
            pass
    with socket.socket() as s:
        s.bind(("127.0.0.1", 0))
        return s.getsockname()[1]


def api(base, path, token=None, method="GET", body=None, timeout=60):
    data = json.dumps(body).encode() if body is not None else None
    req = urllib.request.Request(base.rstrip("/") + "/api/v1" + path, data=data, method=method)
    if data:
        req.add_header("content-type", "application/json")
    if token:
        req.add_header("authorization", "Bearer " + token)
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:
            payload = r.read()
            return json.loads(payload or b"null")
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
        raise Fail(f"{method} {path} -> {e.code}: {e.read().decode(errors='replace')[:200]}")


# ---- fixture media --------------------------------------------------------


def generate_media(media_dir, ffmpeg):
    """Build the fixture corpus once and reuse it forever after.

    Cached, exactly as `plurx_core::testfixtures` caches its own: an encoder is
    only *mostly* reproducible across versions and thread counts, and this gate
    cannot afford "mostly". Reusing the bytes makes the question moot — two runs
    read the same file. The stamp carries the recipe, so changing a duration or
    a codec below regenerates rather than silently keeping stale media.
    """
    recipe = hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(FIXTURES, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()[:16]
    stamp = media_dir / ".recipe"
    if stamp.is_file() and stamp.read_text().strip() == recipe:
        return
    if media_dir.exists():
        shutil.rmtree(media_dir)
    for share, rel, codec, seconds in FIXTURES:
        out = media_dir / share / rel
        out.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        cmd = [
            ffmpeg, "-y", "-v", "error", "-nostdin",
            # `testsrc2` paints a running timecode, which is what makes a paused
            # frame in the player screenshot legible as "we seeked to 2.000s".
            "-f", "lavfi", "-i", f"testsrc2=size=320x180:rate=24:duration={seconds}",
            "-f", "lavfi", "-i", f"sine=frequency=440:sample_rate=48000:duration={seconds}",
        ]
        if codec == "vp9":
            cmd += ["-c:v", "libvpx-vp9", "-b:v", "300k", "-deadline", "realtime",
                    "-cpu-used", "8", "-g", "24", "-c:a", "libopus"]
        else:
            cmd += ["-c:v", "libx264", "-preset", "ultrafast", "-g", "24", "-c:a", "aac"]
        # -threads 1 so a regeneration on a 4-core laptop and a 64-core builder
        # produce the same bytes; slice-threaded x264 does not.
        #
        # `-bitexact` here rather than before the inputs (where it would only
        # configure the demuxers): the Matroska muxer stamps a random SegmentUID
        # and TrackUID into every WebM it writes, so two encodes of byte-identical
        # video differ in their header. This zeroes them, and drops the encoder
        # version string, so a *regenerated* corpus matches a cached one instead
        # of merely being equivalent to it.
        cmd += ["-threads", "1", "-fflags", "+bitexact", "-flags", "+bitexact",
                "-pix_fmt", "yuv420p", "-ac", "2", "-shortest", str(out)]
        subprocess.run(cmd, check=True)
    stamp.write_text(recipe + "\n")


# ---- the throwaway server -------------------------------------------------


class SelfHost:
    """Boot, seed, freeze and serve a plurxd nobody else is using."""

    def __init__(self, args):
        self.args = args
        self.root = Path(args.out).resolve() / "selfhost"
        self.data_dir = self.root / "data"
        # Media lives outside the wiped data dir: it is the expensive part and
        # it never changes. The path itself is rendered (Settings shows a
        # library's paths verbatim), so it is derived from the repo root rather
        # than from a temp dir — same checkout, same string on screen.
        self.media_dir = Path(args.out).resolve() / "media"
        self.config = self.root / "plurx.toml"
        self.log = self.root / "plurxd.log"
        self.port = free_port(args.port)
        self.base_url = f"http://127.0.0.1:{self.port}"
        self.proc = None
        self.token = None
        self.routes = {}

    def server_bin(self):
        if self.args.server_bin:
            path = Path(self.args.server_bin).resolve()
            if not path.exists():
                raise Fail(f"no such server binary: {path}")
            return path
        path = ROOT / "target" / "debug" / "plurxd"
        if not path.exists():
            print("BUILD   cargo build -p plurxd", flush=True)
            subprocess.run(["cargo", "build", "-p", "plurxd"], cwd=ROOT, check=True)
        return path

    def spawn(self):
        handle = open(self.log, "a")
        # start_new_session so a Ctrl-C in the terminal does not race us to the
        # child: this script stops it explicitly, in `stop`.
        self.proc = subprocess.Popen(
            [str(self.server_bin()), "--config", str(self.config), "run"],
            cwd=ROOT, stdout=handle, stderr=handle, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            start_new_session=True,
            env={**os.environ, "PLURX_LOG": "info", "TZ": "UTC"},
        )
        deadline = time.time() + 60
        while time.time() < deadline:
            if self.proc.poll() is not None:
                raise Fail(f"plurxd exited {self.proc.returncode}; see {self.log}")
            try:
                with urllib.request.urlopen(self.base_url + "/healthz", timeout=1) as r:
                    if r.status == 200:
                        return
            except Exception:
                time.sleep(0.2)
        raise Fail(f"plurxd never became healthy; see {self.log}")

    def stop(self):
        if not self.proc or self.proc.poll() is not None:
            return
        self.proc.send_signal(signal.SIGTERM)
        try:
            self.proc.wait(timeout=20)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            self.proc.kill()
            self.proc.wait(timeout=10)

    def start(self):
        generate_media(self.media_dir, self.args.ffmpeg)
        # The database is rebuilt from scratch every run. Reusing it would carry
        # last run's ids, scan results and watch rows into this one, and the
        # first divergence would be invisible until a screenshot moved.
        if self.root.exists():
            shutil.rmtree(self.root)
        self.data_dir.mkdir(parents=True)
        self.config.write_text(
            "# Written by scripts/ui-baseline --self-host. Throwaway.\n"
            f'[server]\nname = "plurx"\nbind = "127.0.0.1:{self.port}"\n\n'
            f'[storage]\ndata_dir = "{self.data_dir}"\n'
        )
        self.spawn()
        self.seed()
        # Restart *after* seeding, and after the timestamps are rewritten. The
        # scan result the server keeps in memory carries the wall-clock instant
        # the scan finished, and Settings and Activity both render it as
        # "· just now" / "· 2m ago" — a stopwatch running against the capture.
        # A fresh process has no such memory, and reads the frozen database.
        self.stop()
        self.freeze_database()
        self.spawn()
        self.discover()

    def seed(self):
        self.token = api(self.base_url, "/setup", method="POST",
                         body={"username": SEED_USER, "password": SEED_PASSWORD})["token"]
        libs = []
        for name, kind, share in LIBRARIES:
            libs.append(api(self.base_url, "/libraries", self.token, "POST", {
                "name": name, "kind": kind,
                "paths": [str(self.media_dir / share)], "anime": False,
            }))
        deadline = time.time() + 300
        while time.time() < deadline:
            status = api(self.base_url, "/scan/status", self.token)
            done = [status.get(str(l["id"])) for l in libs]
            if all(s and not s["running"] and s.get("last_scan") for s in done):
                break
            time.sleep(0.4)
        else:
            raise Fail("the seeded libraries never finished scanning")

        by_title = {}
        for lib in libs:
            page = api(self.base_url, f"/libraries/{lib['id']}/items?limit=200&sort=title", self.token)
            for item in page["items"]:
                by_title[item["title"]] = item
        # Watch state, so Home has a "Continue watching" and a "Next up" rail
        # and the item page has a Resume button — three more relative-time
        # renderings under test, and the difference between photographing the
        # app and photographing an empty one.
        heat = by_title.get("Heat")
        if heat:
            api(self.base_url, f"/items/{heat['id']}/progress", self.token, "POST",
                {"position_ms": 7000, "duration_ms": 12000})
        listing = api(self.base_url, f"/libraries/{libs[2]['id']}/items?limit=200&sort=title", self.token)
        shows = [i for i in listing["items"] if i["kind"] == "show"]
        if shows:
            # show -> seasons -> episodes: the listing only returns shows, so
            # the first episode takes two more hops to reach.
            detail = api(self.base_url, f"/items/{shows[0]['id']}", self.token)
            first = None
            for season in detail.get("children") or []:
                kids = api(self.base_url, f"/items/{season['id']}", self.token).get("children") or []
                if kids:
                    first = sorted(kids, key=lambda e: (e.get("episode_number") or 0))[0]
                    break
            if first:
                # Past the 95% mark, so the store flips it watched and "Next up"
                # advances to episode two — the state a real library is in.
                api(self.base_url, f"/items/{first['id']}/progress", self.token, "POST",
                    {"position_ms": 4900, "duration_ms": 5000})
        self.libs = libs

    def freeze_database(self):
        """Rewrite every stored instant to a constant, backwards from FROZEN_EPOCH.

        The page clock is frozen separately; this is the other half. Freezing
        only the page would render "in 3 days" for a library scanned after the
        frozen instant, and freezing only the database would still leave the
        page measuring from a moving now. Ordering is assigned by title rather
        than by row id: ids come out of the scan in readdir order, which is a
        filesystem property, and a tie in added_at would let two runs sort
        "Recently added" differently.
        """
        db = sqlite3.connect(self.data_dir / "plurx.db")
        try:
            base = FROZEN_EPOCH - 3 * 86400
            rows = list(db.execute(
                "SELECT id FROM items ORDER BY title, season_number, episode_number, id"))
            for n, (item_id,) in enumerate(rows):
                stamp = base - n * 3600
                db.execute("UPDATE items SET added_at = ?, updated_at = ? WHERE id = ?",
                           (stamp, stamp, item_id))
            db.execute("UPDATE files SET scanned_at = ?", (base,))
            db.execute("UPDATE watch_state SET updated_at = ?", (base + 3600,))
            db.execute("UPDATE users SET created_at = ?", (FROZEN_EPOCH - 30 * 86400,))
            db.execute("UPDATE tokens SET created_at = ?, last_seen_at = ?",
                       (FROZEN_EPOCH - 30 * 86400, FROZEN_EPOCH - 30 * 86400))
            db.execute("UPDATE settings SET updated_at = ?", (FROZEN_EPOCH - 30 * 86400,))
            db.execute("UPDATE libraries SET created_at = ?, last_scan_at = ?",
                       (FROZEN_EPOCH - 30 * 86400, base))
            db.commit()
        finally:
            db.close()

    def discover(self):
        self.routes = discover_routes(self.base_url, self.token, self.args,
                                      preferred_library=self.libs[0]["id"],
                                      preferred_item="Heat",
                                      preferred_player="The Signal",
                                      preferred_query="signal")


# ---- route table ----------------------------------------------------------


# What Playwright's Chromium can decode. It is built without the proprietary
# codecs, so H.264/AAC — which is most of a real library, and what the server
# transcodes *to* — plays nowhere in it. The player route therefore needs a
# source in this set, or nothing ever reaches a decoded frame and the route
# times out rather than failing with a reason. A `--browser` pointed at a real
# Chrome or Edge decodes more than this; `--player-item` is the override for
# that case.
BROWSER_VIDEO_CODECS = {"vp9", "vp8", "av1", "av01", "theora"}
# How many item detail lookups the player-item search is allowed. A real
# library has thousands of titles and this runs before the first screenshot;
# past a few dozen misses, asking the operator for --player-item is faster than
# walking the catalogue.
PLAYABLE_SEARCH_LIMIT = 40


def pick_playable(base_url, token, items, preferred):
    """An item the capture browser stands a chance of actually decoding."""
    if preferred and preferred in items:
        return items[preferred]["id"]
    for title in sorted(items)[:PLAYABLE_SEARCH_LIMIT]:
        detail = api(base_url, f"/items/{items[title]['id']}", token)
        for f in detail.get("files") or []:
            if f.get("available") and (f.get("video_codec") or "").lower() in BROWSER_VIDEO_CODECS:
                return items[title]["id"]
    # Not fatal here: a `--browser` with the proprietary codecs will play the
    # fallback fine, and only the player route suffers if it cannot.
    print("warn:   no VP9/AV1 title found for the player route — if it times out, "
          "pass --player-item with something this browser can decode", file=sys.stderr)
    return items[sorted(items)[0]]["id"]


def discover_routes(base_url, token, args, preferred_library=None, preferred_item=None,
                    preferred_player=None, preferred_query=None):
    """Resolve the nine routes against whatever server we were pointed at.

    Ids are looked up rather than hardcoded so `--base-url` works against a real
    library. The ids themselves never appear in a screenshot — only in the hash
    — so a server whose ids differ still produces comparable pictures for the
    same content.
    """
    libs = api(base_url, "/libraries", token)
    if not libs:
        raise Fail("the server has no libraries — nothing to photograph")
    library_id = args.library or preferred_library or sorted(l["id"] for l in libs)[0]
    lib = next((l for l in libs if l["id"] == library_id), libs[0])
    category = {"movies": "movie", "shows": "show", "home": "home"}.get(lib["kind"], lib["kind"])

    items = {}
    for l in libs:
        for item in api(base_url, f"/libraries/{l['id']}/items?limit=200&sort=title", token)["items"]:
            items.setdefault(item["title"], item)
    if not items:
        raise Fail("the server's libraries are empty — nothing to photograph")

    def pick(preferred):
        if preferred and preferred in items:
            return items[preferred]["id"]
        return items[sorted(items)[0]]["id"]

    item_id = args.item or pick(preferred_item)
    player_id = args.player_item or pick_playable(base_url, token, items, preferred_player)
    query = args.search_query or preferred_query or sorted(items)[0].split()[0].lower()

    return {
        "home": {"hash": "#/"},
        "library": {"hash": f"#/library/{library_id}"},
        "category": {"hash": f"#/category/{category}"},
        "search": {"hash": f"#/search/{query}"},
        "item": {"hash": f"#/item/{item_id}"},
        "activity": {"hash": "#/activity"},
        "settings": {"hash": "#/settings"},
        # No token in localStorage, so `boot()` falls through to the sign-in
        # card. The only route whose container is #app: there is no #main
        # until somebody is signed in.
        "auth": {"hash": "#/", "anonymous": True, "container": "#app"},
        "player": {"hash": f"#/item/{player_id}", "player": True},
    }


# ---- capture --------------------------------------------------------------


# A numeric path segment is a row id, and a row id is a fact about this run's
# database rather than about the app. `/api/v1/libraries/3/items` and
# `/api/v1/libraries/1/items` are the same assertion.
API_ID_RE = re.compile(r"/\d+(?=/|$)")


def api_path(path):
    return API_ID_RE.sub("/<id>", path)


def settle(page):
    """Everything that must be true before the shutter opens."""
    # Fonts first: the app embeds its faces as data URLs, so this resolves
    # immediately — but "immediately" is not "already", and a capture that beats
    # it renders a fallback face at different metrics.
    page.evaluate("() => document.fonts.ready")
    # `decode()` rather than `complete`: a cached image reports complete before
    # it is decodable, and Chromium paints nothing for it.
    page.evaluate("""async () => {
      await Promise.all(Array.from(document.images).map(i => i.decode().catch(() => {})));
    }""")
    # The rails remember a horizontal scroll offset per element and the page a
    # vertical one; both survive a hash change within the same document.
    page.evaluate("""() => {
      window.scrollTo(0, 0);
      document.querySelectorAll('.rowscroll, .logbox').forEach(el => { el.scrollLeft = 0; el.scrollTop = 0; });
    }""")
    # Two rAFs: the first lands in the frame currently being built, the second
    # is the first frame that can contain everything above.
    page.evaluate("() => new Promise(r => requestAnimationFrame(() => requestAnimationFrame(r)))")


def open_player(page, timeout_ms):
    """Open the player on the item page and park it on one exact frame.

    The player is the one route with a moving picture in it, and no amount of
    settling makes a playing video deterministic. So it is stopped rather than
    waited on: play for real (a real decision, a real stream — nothing here is
    stubbed), then pause and seek to a fixed offset. `testsrc2` paints its own
    timecode, so the parked frame is the same picture on every run, and the
    transport reads 0:02 because it genuinely is at two seconds.
    """
    page.click("#main button.btnplay", timeout=timeout_ms)
    # readyState 3 (HAVE_FUTURE_DATA) means the decoder has the frame — before
    # that a seek lands wherever the demuxer happens to be.
    try:
        page.wait_for_function(
            "() => { const v = document.getElementById('video'); return v && v.readyState >= 3; }",
            timeout=timeout_ms)
    except Exception:
        # Overwhelmingly this is a codec the capture browser cannot decode, and
        # a bare "wait_for_function timed out" sends the reader looking in the
        # wrong place. Report what the player itself said.
        state = page.evaluate("""() => {
          const t = document.getElementById('ploadText');
          const s = document.getElementById('ploadSub');
          return ((t && t.textContent) || '') + ((s && s.textContent) ? ' — ' + s.textContent : '');
        }""")
        raise Fail("the player never produced a decodable frame"
                   + (f" (player says: {state.strip()})" if state.strip() else "")
                   + " — pass --player-item with a title this browser can decode")
    page.evaluate("""async () => {
      const v = document.getElementById('video');
      v.pause();
      await new Promise(r => { v.addEventListener('seeked', r, { once: true }); v.currentTime = 2.0; });
      // The chrome auto-hides after 2.6s of playing. It cannot re-hide while
      // paused (the idle timer checks), but it may already have hidden during
      // start-up, so the class is cleared explicitly.
      const p = document.getElementById('player');
      if (p) p.classList.remove('idle');
    }""")
    # The toast the player raises on a state change fades on its own timer.
    # Waiting it out is cheaper than deciding whether it is "supposed" to be
    # in the picture — either answer is fine, only a race is not.
    page.wait_for_function(
        "() => { const t = document.getElementById('toast'); return !t || !t.classList.contains('on'); }",
        timeout=timeout_ms)


def freeze_timers(page):
    """Stop every pending timer, so the page cannot repaint under the recorders.

    The app polls activity every 4s and re-renders on the answer. A re-render
    that lands in the middle of the tab walk — which is ~30 round trips, so it
    happens — either destroys the element focus was about to reach (one stop
    silently missing) or blows focus back to <body> (the walk reads that as
    "wrapped" and stops early). Both were observed, on the same route, on two
    consecutive runs of this script, and both are the harness moving rather than
    the app: the answer to "what is tabbable here" must not depend on which side
    of a poll the shutter closed.

    Called after `--settle-ms` has already bought the steady state, so nothing
    still-pending is load work: what is left is the pollers, and the pollers can
    only move the page away from the state that was settled for.

    Chromium hands out timer ids from one increasing pool per document, so the
    id of a timer created now is an upper bound on every id issued before it.
    Clearing the whole range is blunt and complete; enumerating them is not
    possible from script.
    """
    page.evaluate("""() => {
      const top = setTimeout(() => {}, 0);
      for (let i = 0; i <= top; i++) { clearTimeout(i); clearInterval(i); }
    }""")


# Undo everything a tab walk did to the page: the focus ring, and the rails and
# document it scrolled while walking. Run before the shutter, so the picture is
# still of the page at rest.
UNWALK_JS = """
() => {
  if (document.activeElement && document.activeElement.blur) document.activeElement.blur();
  window.scrollTo(0, 0);
  document.querySelectorAll('.rowscroll, .logbox').forEach(el => { el.scrollLeft = 0; el.scrollTop = 0; });
}
"""


def tab_order(page, stops):
    """Press Tab from a known start and record who takes focus, in order.

    The rail scroll is reset before every press, not once at the start. Focusing
    a card scrolls its rail, and the rail's own arrows are shown by a class the
    scroll handler toggles (`.railbox.can-l/.can-r`) — so walking the page
    changes which elements are tabbable AS IT WALKS, and whether Tab reached the
    right-hand arrow before the rail scrolled past needing it came out different
    on consecutive runs of an unchanged tree. Resetting first means every press
    resolves against a rail at rest, which is also the only state a reader of
    this dump could reasonably picture.
    """
    page.evaluate("""() => {
      // A known start: whatever the page focused on load is discarded, so the
      // first Tab always lands on the first tabbable element in DOM order.
      if (document.activeElement && document.activeElement.blur) document.activeElement.blur();
    }""")
    seen = []
    for _ in range(stops):
        page.evaluate("""() => {
          document.querySelectorAll('.rowscroll').forEach(el => { el.scrollLeft = 0; });
        }""")
        page.keyboard.press("Tab")
        descriptor = page.evaluate(FOCUS_JS)
        if descriptor is None:
            # Focus left the document — the tab ring wrapped through the
            # browser's own chrome. That is the end of the page's order.
            seen.append("<wrap>")
            break
        seen.append(descriptor)
    return seen


def capture_route(pw_context, base_url, name, route, args, shot, layout_id):
    page = pw_context.new_page()
    problems = []
    # The request log. Four documents cite a "request-log invariant" — converting
    # a route, or wearing a different layout, must not change which endpoints it
    # calls or how many times — and until now nothing measured it. A layout that
    # quietly fetches is the single failure the whole seam exists to prevent, and
    # it is invisible in a screenshot: the page looks right and costs twice.
    #
    # Method + path only, query string dropped and numeric path segments
    # replaced: ids and paging offsets are fixture-dependent and would make the
    # log a second golden nobody can read. The ids are not incidental noise, they
    # are actively wrong to record — the seeded database is rebuilt every run and
    # item ids come out of the scan in readdir order, so `GET /api/v1/items/6`
    # and `GET /api/v1/items/7` are the same request about the same film on two
    # runs an hour apart. (Observed: the first --check on an unchanged tree
    # reported twelve request "drifts" that were entirely this.) The invariant is
    # WHICH endpoints a route calls and HOW MANY times, never which row.
    #
    # Sorted and counted rather than kept in order, because Promise.all settles
    # in whatever order the network feels like and an ordering diff would be
    # noise, not signal.
    api_calls = []
    page.on("request", lambda r: api_calls.append(
        f"{r.method} {api_path(urlparse(r.url).path)}") if "/api/" in urlparse(r.url).path else None)
    # Console errors and uncaught exceptions are collected for the whole visit,
    # not sampled at the end: an error thrown during boot is exactly the kind
    # this gate exists to catch, and it is long gone by the time we screenshot.
    page.on("console", lambda m: problems.append(f"console.{m.type}: {m.text}") if m.type == "error" else None)
    page.on("pageerror", lambda e: problems.append(f"pageerror: {e}"))
    try:
        response = page.goto(base_url + "/" + route["hash"], wait_until="networkidle",
                             timeout=args.timeout)
        if response is not None and not response.ok:
            raise Fail(f"{name}: document responded {response.status}")

        if args.self_test_fail == "http" and name == args.self_test_route:
            page.goto(base_url + "/definitely-not-a-page", wait_until="domcontentloaded")
            raise Fail(f"{name}: document responded 404")

        container = route.get("container", "#main")
        page.wait_for_selector(container, state="attached", timeout=args.timeout)
        # The layout the app actually resolved, not the one localStorage asked
        # for. `layoutId()` falls back to classic on a surface a layout does not
        # declare, and the sweep already skipped those pairs — so a mismatch
        # here means this script's model of surface resolution is wrong, and
        # every capture after it would be filed under a layout that never ran.
        # Loud is the only safe answer; a fallback photographed under the name
        # of the thing that fell back is worse than no photograph.
        got = page.evaluate("() => layoutId()")
        if got != layout_id:
            raise Fail(f"{name}: asked for layout {layout_id!r}, the app resolved {got!r} "
                       "— its `surfaces` and this viewport disagree")
        # The app polls activity every 4s and the activity page re-renders every
        # 3s. Rather than stubbing either — the baseline has to be of the real
        # app — the capture waits out more than one full period, so it always
        # lands on the same steady state instead of on whichever half of the
        # cycle happened to be showing.
        page.wait_for_timeout(args.settle_ms)
        if route.get("player"):
            open_player(page, args.timeout)

        if args.self_test_fail and name == args.self_test_route:
            if args.self_test_fail == "console":
                page.evaluate("() => console.error('ui-baseline self test')")
            elif args.self_test_fail == "pageerror":
                page.evaluate("() => { setTimeout(() => { throw new Error('ui-baseline self test'); }, 0); }")
            elif args.self_test_fail == "empty":
                page.evaluate("(sel) => { document.querySelector(sel).innerHTML = ''; }", container)
            page.wait_for_timeout(200)

        settle(page)

        empty = page.evaluate("""(sel) => {
          const el = document.querySelector(sel);
          if (!el) return true;
          return el.childElementCount === 0 && !(el.textContent || '').trim();
        }""", container)
        if empty:
            raise Fail(f"{name}: {container} rendered empty")

        # Order matters here, and it is the opposite of the obvious one.
        #
        # A full-page screenshot resizes the viewport to the document to capture
        # below the fold, and Chromium does not restore the context's TOUCH
        # emulation afterwards. So a page that reported `(hover: none)` before
        # the shutter reports `(hover: hover)` after it — and the rail arrows,
        # which are `display:none` outside `@media(hover:hover)`, become
        # tabbable elements that a phone would never have. Measuring after the
        # shutter measured a device that does not exist, and did it about half
        # the time. Everything that must describe THIS surface therefore runs
        # first, and the shutter is last.
        #
        # `#modal` is added only for the player route — it holds the player
        # chrome, which is static markup outside `#app`, and adding it
        # everywhere would repeat ninety identical lines in thirty-six captures
        # to assert nothing.
        freeze_timers(page)
        roots = ["#app"] + (["#modal"] if route.get("player") else [])
        dom = page.evaluate(DOM_JS, roots)
        focus = tab_order(page, args.tab_stops)
        # The walk left a focus ring and a scrolled rail behind it; the picture
        # is of the page at rest, so put it back before the shutter.
        #
        # The wait is not padding. Resetting `scrollLeft` fires a scroll event,
        # and the app's handler toggles `.railbox.can-l/.can-r` — which is what
        # shows or hides the rail arrows. Scroll events are delivered at a frame
        # boundary, so a screenshot taken straight after the reset catches the
        # arrows in whichever state the race left them: nine of thirty-six
        # captures moved between two runs of an unchanged tree until this was
        # here, all of them rail routes.
        page.evaluate(UNWALK_JS)
        page.wait_for_timeout(args.rest_ms)
        settle(page)
        page.screenshot(path=str(shot), full_page=not args.viewport_only)
        if problems:
            raise Fail(f"{name}: " + "; ".join(problems[:5]))
        counted = {}
        for call in api_calls:
            counted[call] = counted.get(call, 0) + 1
        return focus, dict(sorted(counted.items())), dom
    finally:
        page.close()


def context_prefs(args, layout_id, token, route):
    """Every UI preference the app persists, written before the app's first line.

    Theme, light/dark, poster size, Home grouping, page size, Settings tab. Any
    one of them left at "whatever the default is today" would silently
    redecorate the baseline the day that default changes.
    """
    prefs = {
        # The layout is a baseline dimension exactly like the theme: the same
        # routes have to be photographed under every layout, and a layout
        # applied after first paint would capture the wrong chrome.
        "plurx_layout": layout_id,
        "plurx_theme": args.theme,
        "plurx_appearance": args.appearance,
        "plurx_iconsize": "s",
        "plurx_home_group": "category",
        "plurx_settings_tab": "libraries",
        "plurx_quality": "auto",
        "plurx_playerinfo": "1",
        "plurx_autoskip": "0",
        "plurx_autonext": "1",
        # The install banner is dismissible and its state is sticky; pinning it
        # dismissed keeps Home's first row from depending on the browser's PWA
        # opinion.
        "plurx_getapp_dismissed": "1",
    }
    if not route.get("anonymous"):
        prefs["plurx_token"] = token
    return prefs


def new_context(browser, args, vp, layout_id, token, route):
    """One browsing context, pinned on every axis that could move a capture."""
    context = browser.new_context(
        viewport={"width": vp["width"], "height": vp["height"]},
        # Touch capability drives `surfaceClass()`; see VIEWPORTS. `is_mobile`
        # is deliberately NOT set: it turns on viewport-meta emulation and an
        # overlay scrollbar, which changes the picture for reasons that have
        # nothing to do with the layout under test.
        has_touch=vp["touch"],
        # Retina would double every pixel and hide nothing; 1 is also the only
        # ratio that is the same on both machines.
        device_scale_factor=1,
        locale="en-US",
        timezone_id="UTC",
        # The app asks matchMedia for both of these. Pinning them stops the
        # host's own accessibility and appearance settings from leaking into
        # the picture.
        reduced_motion="reduce",
        color_scheme="dark",
    )
    context.add_init_script(
        FREEZE_JS.replace("__FROZEN_MS__", str(FROZEN_EPOCH * 1000))
                 .replace("__FREEZE_CSS__", json.dumps(FREEZE_CSS))
    )
    context.add_init_script(
        "(() => { try { const p = %s; for (const k in p) localStorage.setItem(k, p[k]); } catch (e) {} })();"
        % json.dumps(context_prefs(args, layout_id, token, route))
    )
    return context


def read_registry(browser, base_url, token, args):
    """Ask the running app which layouts exist, and what each viewport IS.

    One page load per viewport, before any capture, for two answers that cannot
    be derived anywhere else honestly: `Object.keys(LAYOUTS)` with each layout's
    declared `surfaces`, and the `surfaceClass()` this viewport resolves to.
    Both come from the page because both are the app's own decisions — a copy of
    either kept in this file would be right until the day it silently was not,
    which is the failure R1 exists to end.
    """
    layouts = None
    surfaces = {}
    probe = {"hash": "#/"}
    for vp in VIEWPORTS:
        context = new_context(browser, args, vp, "classic", token, probe)
        try:
            page = context.new_page()
            page.goto(base_url + "/#/", wait_until="networkidle", timeout=args.timeout)
            # The <head> registry only carries `classic`; every later layout
            # attaches itself from the body script. Waiting for #app to have
            # content is waiting for that script to have finished.
            page.wait_for_selector("#app", state="attached", timeout=args.timeout)
            info = page.evaluate(REGISTRY_JS)
            surfaces[vp_key(vp)] = info["surface"]
            if layouts is None:
                layouts = info["layouts"]
            page.close()
        finally:
            context.close()
    return layouts, surfaces


def vp_key(vp):
    return f"{vp['width']}x{vp['height']}"


def plan(layouts, surfaces, routes, wanted):
    """Every (layout, viewport, route) triple this app would actually render.

    A layout is skipped at a viewport whose surface it does not declare, because
    `layoutId()` would fall back to classic there and the capture would be a
    duplicate of classic filed under the wrong name — which is precisely the
    "the harness answered the question it was asked" failure of §3. Skips are
    printed, not swallowed: a layout that quietly captures nothing is a layout
    with no gate.
    """
    known = {L["id"]: L for L in layouts}
    if wanted == ["all"]:
        chosen = sorted(known)
    else:
        missing = [w for w in wanted if w not in known]
        if missing:
            raise Fail(f"no such layout(s): {', '.join(missing)} — the app registers "
                       f"{', '.join(sorted(known))}")
        chosen = sorted(dict.fromkeys(wanted))
    triples, skipped = [], []
    for layout_id in chosen:
        for vp in VIEWPORTS:
            surface = surfaces[vp_key(vp)]
            if surface not in known[layout_id]["surfaces"]:
                skipped.append(f"{layout_id} @ {vp_key(vp)}: layout declares no "
                               f"{surface!r} surface")
                continue
            for name, route in routes.items():
                triples.append((layout_id, vp, name, route))
    return triples, skipped


def run_capture(base_url, token, routes, args, out_dir):
    from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright

    executable = find_browser(args.browser)
    focus_dump = {}
    request_dump = {}
    dom_dump = {}
    pixels = {}
    failures = []
    with sync_playwright() as pw:
        browser = pw.chromium.launch(
            executable_path=executable,
            args=[
                # No sandbox because this runs as root in a container; harmless
                # on a Mac, where Playwright's own Chromium is already unsandboxed
                # for automation.
                "--no-sandbox",
                "--disable-dev-shm-usage",
                # Software rendering only. A GPU raster path differs per host and
                # per driver, which is precisely the kind of "identical tree,
                # different pixels" this gate must not have.
                "--disable-gpu",
                # A scrollbar appears on some routes and not others, and its
                # width shifts the whole content box. Hiding it removes a
                # difference that is about content length, not layout.
                "--hide-scrollbars",
                "--force-device-scale-factor=1",
                "--force-color-profile=srgb",
                # Font rasterisation: hinting and subpixel positioning are both
                # host-configurable, and LCD text bakes the subpixel order of a
                # display into the PNG.
                "--font-render-hinting=none",
                "--disable-lcd-text",
                "--disable-font-subpixel-positioning",
                # A backgrounded renderer throttles timers, so the app's pollers
                # fire late and the "settled" state is not the settled state.
                "--disable-background-timer-throttling",
                "--disable-renderer-backgrounding",
                "--disable-backgrounding-occluded-windows",
                "--lang=en-US",
            ],
        )
        try:
            layouts, surfaces = read_registry(browser, base_url, token, args)
            print("LAYOUTS " + ", ".join(f"{L['id']} ({'/'.join(L['surfaces'])})"
                                         for L in layouts), flush=True)
            print("SURFACE " + ", ".join(f"{k}={v}" for k, v in sorted(surfaces.items())),
                  flush=True)
            triples, skipped = plan(layouts, surfaces, routes, args.layout)
            for line in skipped:
                print(f"SKIP    {line}", flush=True)
            # Last run's PNGs for these layouts, removed before this run writes
            # any: a stale capture from a route table that no longer exists
            # would sit beside the new ones and quietly pass a hash comparison
            # nobody re-derived. Scoped to the layout directories so the cached
            # `media/` corpus and the `selfhost/` data dir survive.
            for layout_id in sorted({t[0] for t in triples}):
                shutil.rmtree(out_dir / layout_id, ignore_errors=True)
                (out_dir / layout_id).mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)

            for layout_id, vp, name, route in triples:
                context = new_context(browser, args, vp, layout_id, token, route)
                key = f"{layout_id}|{name}@{vp_key(vp)}"
                shot = out_dir / layout_id / f"{name}@{vp_key(vp)}.png"
                try:
                    focus_dump[key], request_dump[key], dom_dump[key] = capture_route(
                        context, base_url, name, route, args, shot, layout_id)
                    pixels[key] = hashlib.sha256(shot.read_bytes()).hexdigest()
                    print(f"OK      {key}", flush=True)
                except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 — every failure is a gate failure
                    failures.append(f"{key}: {e}")
                    print(f"FAIL    {key}: {e}", flush=True)
                finally:
                    context.close()
        finally:
            browser.close()
    return {
        "layouts": layouts,
        "surfaces": surfaces,
        "focus": focus_dump,
        "requests": request_dump,
        "dom": dom_dump,
        "pixels": pixels,
        "failures": failures,
    }


# ---- the portable golden --------------------------------------------------


# Absolute paths that reach a rendered string. Settings prints a library's
# paths verbatim, and the seeded library lives under `--out` — so the same tree
# checked out twice, or run twice at different `--out` values, would produce
# two different goldens and the committed one would be a fact about one
# directory.
#
# The catch-all is not there to handle the paths we know about (those are named
# in `path_tokens` and replaced first, longest-first so `<out>/media` wins over
# `<out>`). It is there so a path we did NOT anticipate cannot quietly make the
# golden machine-specific. A false positive costs a slightly vaguer golden line
# — the Settings placeholder `/path/to/media` reads `<path>` and nobody is
# worse off; a false negative costs everyone else a red gate they cannot fix.
#
# No space in the character class: a label like "Light / dark" must survive, and
# the real paths this run can produce are already named above.
ABS_PATH_RE = re.compile(r"(?:[A-Za-z]:)?[/\\](?:[\w.@+-]+[/\\]){2,}[\w.@+-]*")


def scrub(text, replacements):
    """Remove anything host- or path-dependent from a rendered STRING.

    Applied to free text only — a focus label. Not to a DOM signature or a
    request path: `/api/v1/libraries` looks exactly like a filesystem path to
    any regex, and scrubbing it would delete the request-log invariant (R2)
    while appearing to work.
    """
    for needle, token in replacements:
        if needle:
            text = text.replace(str(needle), token)
    return ABS_PATH_RE.sub("<path>", text)


def path_tokens(args, out_dir):
    """The concrete strings this run knows are local to this machine."""
    return [
        (str(Path(out_dir).resolve() / "media"), "<media>"),
        (str(Path(out_dir).resolve()), "<out>"),
        (str(ROOT), "<root>"),
        (str(Path.home()), "<home>"),
    ]


# How much of a focus label survives into the golden. Enough to tell two
# controls apart, little enough that a long description is not three lines of
# diff; applied AFTER scrubbing, so the cut never lands inside a path.
FOCUS_LABEL_CHARS = 60


def structure_lines(result, args, out_dir):
    """The portable tier as sorted `layout|capture|kind|key|value` lines.

    One logical fact per line, so a reviewer reads a pull request diff instead
    of a whole file: a lost `unw` class is one `-` and one `+`, naming the
    layout, the route, the viewport and the selector. JSON would nest the same
    facts three levels deep and turn every change into a re-indented block.

    `|` is the separator and `value` is the last field, so only the first four
    fields must avoid it; a `|` that reaches a key is escaped rather than
    trusted, because a focus label is app text and app text is not this
    script's to promise anything about.
    """
    sub = path_tokens(args, out_dir)

    def key(s):
        """A DOM signature or a request path: structural, never free text."""
        return str(s).replace("|", "¦").replace("\n", " ")

    def text(s):
        """A focus label: app text, so scrub it, THEN cut it (see FOCUS_JS)."""
        return scrub(str(s), sub)[:FOCUS_LABEL_CHARS].replace("|", "¦").replace("\n", " ")

    lines = []
    # Meta first (`*` sorts before every layout id): the registry itself is
    # under test. A layout added without regenerating the golden, or a layout
    # whose `surfaces` changed, is then a one-line diff at the top of the file
    # rather than three hundred lines of captures further down that nobody
    # traces back to a cause.
    for L in result["layouts"]:
        lines.append(f"*|*|layout|{key(L['id'])}|{' '.join(L['surfaces'])}")
    for vp, surface in sorted(result["surfaces"].items()):
        lines.append(f"*|*|surface|{key(vp)}|{surface}")

    for cap, dom in result["dom"].items():
        layout, capture = cap.split("|", 1)
        for sig, count in dom.items():
            lines.append(f"{layout}|{capture}|dom|{key(sig)}|{count}")
    for cap, focus in result["focus"].items():
        layout, capture = cap.split("|", 1)
        # Zero-padded so the tab ORDER survives a lexicographic sort — an
        # order that sorts 10 before 2 is not an order.
        for n, descriptor in enumerate(focus):
            lines.append(f"{layout}|{capture}|focus|{n:03d}|{text(descriptor)}")
    for cap, requests in result["requests"].items():
        layout, capture = cap.split("|", 1)
        for call, count in requests.items():
            lines.append(f"{layout}|{capture}|req|{key(call)}|{count}")
    return sorted(lines)


GOLDEN_HEADER = """\
# plurx UI structural golden — the portable tier of scripts/ui-baseline.
#
#   regenerate   scripts/ui-baseline --self-host --update
#   verify       make ui-check
#
# COMMITTED ON PURPOSE, and pixel-free on purpose. A PNG hash depends on the
# Chromium build, the rasteriser and the path the fixture library sits at, so a
# committed pixel manifest is red on every machine but the one that wrote it.
# Everything below is a fact about the DOM the app builds, and is therefore the
# same on a Mac, in the sandbox and on a CI runner. Pixel hashes stay in
# target/ui-baseline/pixels.txt and are compared with --baseline, never here.
#
# Format, one fact per line:
#
#   <layout>|<route>@<w>x<h>|<kind>|<key>|<value>
#
#   layout   dark|light etc. are NOT dimensions here — one theme, every layout.
#            `*` marks a fact about the registry rather than about a capture.
#   kind     layout   a registered layout and the surfaces it declares
#            surface  which surfaceClass() a viewport resolves to
#            dom      a structural signature `parent > self`, and how many of
#                     them the route rendered. Tags, ids, sorted classes, role,
#                     tabindex, ARIA state and a bare `text` flag — never text
#                     content, inline style, href, src or value.
#            focus    the Nth tab stop, zero-padded so the order sorts
#            req      an API call the route made, method + path, and how often
#
# Regeneration must be a deliberate act with a diff, never a side effect of a
# normal run — that is why `--update` exists and why a plain run writes only
# into target/. If this file changed and you did not mean it to, the app
# changed.
"""


def write_golden(path, lines):
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    path.write_text(GOLDEN_HEADER + "\n".join(lines) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")


def parse_golden(text):
    """Golden text to {(layout, capture, kind, key): value}, comments dropped."""
    out = {}
    for raw in text.splitlines():
        if not raw or raw.startswith("#"):
            continue
        parts = raw.split("|", 4)
        if len(parts) != 5:
            raise Fail(f"malformed golden line (expected 5 |-separated fields): {raw[:120]}")
        out[tuple(parts[:4])] = parts[4]
    return out


def diff_golden(want, got, limit):
    """A per-key report of what moved, grouped so a human can act on it.

    Grouped by (layout, capture) because that is the unit somebody debugs: the
    first question about a drift is always "which layout, which route, which
    viewport", and a flat list of five hundred keys answers it last. A capture
    that is wholly absent on one side collapses to a single line — a new layout
    must not print three hundred additions and bury the one fact that matters.
    """
    want_caps = {k[:2] for k in want}
    got_caps = {k[:2] for k in got}
    report, changed = [], 0

    for cap in sorted(want_caps - got_caps):
        report.append(f"  - {cap[0]} | {cap[1]}: captured in the golden, MISSING from this run")
        changed += 1
    for cap in sorted(got_caps - want_caps):
        report.append(f"  + {cap[0]} | {cap[1]}: captured by this run, ABSENT from the golden")
        changed += 1

    shared = want_caps & got_caps
    for cap in sorted(shared):
        w = {k[2:]: v for k, v in want.items() if k[:2] == cap}
        g = {k[2:]: v for k, v in got.items() if k[:2] == cap}
        rows = []
        for k in sorted(set(w) | set(g)):
            if k not in g:
                rows.append(f"      - {k[0]:5} {k[1]}   was {w[k]}")
            elif k not in w:
                rows.append(f"      + {k[0]:5} {k[1]}   now {g[k]}")
            elif w[k] != g[k]:
                rows.append(f"      ~ {k[0]:5} {k[1]}   {w[k]} -> {g[k]}")
        if rows:
            changed += len(rows)
            report.append(f"  {cap[0]} | {cap[1]}")
            report.extend(rows)

    truncated = []
    if len(report) > limit:
        truncated = report[:limit] + [f"  … {len(report) - limit} more lines "
                                      "(raise --diff-lines to see them all)"]
    return changed, (truncated or report)


def compare_pixels(pixels, baseline_dir):
    """Which captures moved since a previous LOCAL run. Never a committed check.

    The pixel tier answers one question — "I refactored; did any picture
    change?" — and it can only answer it between two runs on one machine. So it
    is a directory you point at, not a file in git; there is no way to make a
    PNG hash mean the same thing on two hosts, and pretending otherwise is how
    a gate gets muted.
    """
    ref = Path(baseline_dir).resolve() / "pixels.txt"
    if not ref.is_file():
        raise Fail(f"--baseline {baseline_dir}: no pixels.txt there — that directory is "
                   "not a previous ui-baseline run")
    was = {}
    for line in ref.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines():
        if line and not line.startswith("#"):
            digest, _, key = line.partition("  ")
            was[key] = digest
    moved, added, gone = [], [], []
    for key in sorted(set(was) | set(pixels)):
        if key not in was:
            added.append(key)
        elif key not in pixels:
            gone.append(key)
        elif was[key] != pixels[key]:
            moved.append(key)
    return moved, added, gone


def write_pixels(path, pixels):
    # `sha256sum`-compatible ("<digest>  <name>"), so the file can be checked
    # with the tool everybody already reaches for, and diffed as plain text.
    path.write_text("".join(f"{v}  {k}\n" for k, v in sorted(pixels.items())),
                    encoding="utf-8")


# ---- entry point ----------------------------------------------------------


def parse_args(argv):
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="scripts/ui-baseline",
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
        description=__doc__,
    )
    p.add_argument("--self-host", action="store_true",
                   help="boot a throwaway plurxd with a seeded library and drive that")
    p.add_argument("--base-url", help="capture an already-running server instead")
    p.add_argument("--token", help="bearer token for --base-url (localStorage.plurx_token)")
    p.add_argument("--out", default=str(ROOT / "target" / "ui-baseline"),
                   help="output directory (default: target/ui-baseline)")
    p.add_argument("--layout", default="all",
                   help="comma-separated layout ids, or 'all' — every layout the app "
                        "registers at runtime (default: all)")
    p.add_argument("--check", action="store_true",
                   help="capture, then compare the portable tier against the committed "
                        "golden and exit nonzero on any drift")
    p.add_argument("--update", action="store_true",
                   help="rewrite the committed golden (%s) — a deliberate act with a diff"
                        % GOLDEN.relative_to(ROOT))
    p.add_argument("--golden", default=str(GOLDEN),
                   help=f"the committed golden to check against (default: {GOLDEN.relative_to(ROOT)})")
    p.add_argument("--baseline",
                   help="a previous run's --out directory; additionally compare the PIXEL "
                        "tier against it and report which captures moved")
    p.add_argument("--diff-lines", type=int, default=120,
                   help="how many drift lines --check prints before truncating (default: 120)")
    p.add_argument("--theme", default="noirr", help="localStorage.plurx_theme (default: noirr)")
    p.add_argument("--appearance", default="dark",
                   help="localStorage.plurx_appearance (default: dark)")
    p.add_argument("--viewport-only", action="store_true",
                   help="capture the viewport instead of the full scrollable page")
    p.add_argument("--tab-stops", type=int, default=60,
                   help="how many times to press Tab per route (default: 60)")
    p.add_argument("--settle-ms", type=int, default=4500,
                   help="quiet period before the shutter, in ms — must exceed the 4s "
                        "activity poll so the capture never lands mid-cycle (default: 4500)")
    p.add_argument("--rest-ms", type=int, default=250,
                   help="quiet period between undoing the tab walk and the shutter, in ms "
                        "— long enough for the rails' scroll handler to have run (default: 250)")
    p.add_argument("--timeout", type=int, default=45000, help="per-step timeout in ms")
    p.add_argument("--browser", help="path to a Chromium binary (default: find one, else Playwright's)")
    p.add_argument("--server-bin", help="plurxd binary for --self-host (default: target/debug/plurxd)")
    p.add_argument("--ffmpeg", default=os.environ.get("PLURX_FFMPEG", "ffmpeg"),
                   help="ffmpeg used to build the fixture media")
    p.add_argument("--port", type=int, default=39247, help="--self-host port, if free")
    p.add_argument("--keep-server", action="store_true",
                   help="leave the throwaway server running so you can poke at it")
    p.add_argument("--library", type=int, help="library id for the library route")
    p.add_argument("--item", type=int, help="item id for the item-detail route")
    p.add_argument("--player-item", type=int, help="item id for the player route")
    p.add_argument("--search-query", help="query for the search route")
    p.add_argument("--self-test-fail", choices=["console", "pageerror", "empty", "http"],
                   help="deliberately break one route to prove the gate exits nonzero")
    p.add_argument("--self-test-route", default="home",
                   help="which route --self-test-fail breaks (default: home)")
    args = p.parse_args(argv)
    if not args.self_host and not args.base_url:
        p.error("pass --self-host, or --base-url (with --token) for a running server")
    if args.base_url and not args.token:
        p.error("--base-url needs --token: every route but auth is behind a login")
    if args.check and args.update:
        p.error("--check and --update are opposites: one asserts the golden, the other "
                "replaces it")
    # A golden is only meaningful against the fixture library it was written
    # from. A real server has different titles, different counts and a different
    # DOM, so --check against --base-url would fail for reasons that are not
    # regressions, every time — and a gate that always fails is a gate that gets
    # deleted.
    if (args.check or args.update) and not args.self_host:
        p.error("--check/--update need --self-host: the golden is written against the "
                "seeded fixture library, and a real server has different content")
    args.layout = [s.strip() for s in args.layout.split(",") if s.strip()]
    if not args.layout:
        p.error("--layout needs at least one id, or 'all'")
    return args


def preflight(args):
    """Fail in the first second, with the fix, or not at all.

    Every requirement below used to be discovered where it was first USED —
    which for playwright meant line ~730, inside run_capture, after this script
    had already generated a fixture corpus with ffmpeg, seeded a SQLite
    database, rewritten every timestamp in it and booted a server. Someone on a
    fresh machine paid for all of that to be told `No module named
    'playwright'`. A dependency check that runs after the expensive part is not
    a check, it is a receipt.

    Everything here is cheap: an import, a `which`, a directory glob. The rule
    for adding to it is that the requirement must be knowable before any work
    starts and must name the command that fixes it — an error that says what is
    missing but not how to get it just moves the search to a browser tab.
    """
    problems = []

    try:
        import playwright  # noqa: F401
    except ImportError:
        problems.append(
            "python module 'playwright' is missing\n"
            "      pip install playwright && python3 -m playwright install chromium\n"
            "      (macOS/Homebrew python refuses --user with 'externally-managed-environment';\n"
            "       then: python3 -m venv ~/.venvs/plurx-ui"
            " && ~/.venvs/plurx-ui/bin/pip install playwright\n"
            "             && ~/.venvs/plurx-ui/bin/playwright install chromium,\n"
            "       and run this script with ~/.venvs/plurx-ui/bin/python)"
        )
    else:
        # An explicit --browser is the operator's claim, so check it rather than
        # trusting it; a resolved path from find_browser() came from a glob that
        # already proved the file exists. None means "let Playwright resolve its
        # own", which is right on a Mac and cannot be verified from here without
        # launching it — that failure is loud and immediate anyway.
        if args.browser and not Path(args.browser).exists():
            problems.append(f"--browser {args.browser} does not exist")
        elif not args.browser and find_browser(None) is None:
            try:
                from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright
                with sync_playwright() as pw:
                    exe = pw.chromium.executable_path
                if not Path(exe).exists():
                    raise FileNotFoundError(exe)
            except Exception:
                problems.append(
                    "playwright is installed but its chromium is not\n"
                    "      python3 -m playwright install chromium"
                )

    # Only --self-host builds fixtures and boots a server; --base-url drives a
    # server somebody else is already running and needs neither.
    if args.self_host:
        if shutil.which(args.ffmpeg) is None:
            problems.append(
                f"ffmpeg not found on PATH as {args.ffmpeg!r} (fixture media is generated with it)\n"
                "      brew install ffmpeg   |   apt install ffmpeg   |   --ffmpeg /path/to/ffmpeg"
            )
        if args.server_bin and not Path(args.server_bin).exists():
            problems.append(f"--server-bin {args.server_bin} does not exist")
        elif not args.server_bin and shutil.which("cargo") is None \
                and not (ROOT / "target" / "debug" / "plurxd").exists():
            problems.append(
                "no target/debug/plurxd and no cargo to build one\n"
                "      cargo build -p plurxd   |   --server-bin /path/to/plurxd"
            )
    else:
        if not args.base_url:
            problems.append("neither --self-host nor --base-url given; there is nothing to capture")

    # Knowable before anything is captured, and each one costs a three-minute
    # sweep if it is discovered afterwards instead.
    if args.check and not Path(args.golden).is_file():
        problems.append(
            f"--check but no golden at {args.golden}\n"
            "      scripts/ui-baseline --self-host --update   (then review the diff and commit it)"
        )
    if args.update and not Path(args.golden).parent.is_dir():
        problems.append(f"--update but {Path(args.golden).parent} does not exist")
    if args.baseline and not (Path(args.baseline) / "pixels.txt").is_file():
        problems.append(
            f"--baseline {args.baseline} has no pixels.txt — it is not a previous run's --out\n"
            f"      scripts/ui-baseline --self-host --out {args.baseline}   (before your change)"
        )
    # This run overwrites its own pixels.txt, so pointing --baseline at --out
    # compares a run against itself and reports "identical" whatever happened —
    # the one answer that is never information.
    if args.baseline and Path(args.baseline).resolve() == Path(args.out).resolve():
        problems.append(
            "--baseline and --out are the same directory; this run would overwrite the\n"
            "      reference before comparing against it. Capture the BEFORE state into a\n"
            "      second directory: scripts/ui-baseline --self-host --out target/ui-before"
        )

    if problems:
        raise Fail(
            "preflight failed, nothing was run:\n  - " + "\n  - ".join(problems)
        )


def main(argv):
    # TZ before anything else, and before the server is spawned: SQLite's
    # unixepoch(), the daemon's own formatting and Python's own time all read
    # it, and a machine in Europe/London would otherwise produce a different
    # baseline from the same code.
    os.environ["TZ"] = "UTC"
    time.tzset()

    args = parse_args(argv)
    # Before the output directory is touched: a preflight that has already
    # deleted last run's goldens is not free to fail. Reported the same way as
    # every other Fail — a missing dependency is an operator problem, and a
    # Python traceback buries the one line that says how to fix it.
    try:
        preflight(args)
    except Fail as e:
        print(f"error: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    out_dir = Path(args.out).resolve()
    out_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    # Legacy layout of this directory: before the sweep, PNGs sat at the top
    # level rather than under <layout>/. Left behind they are stale pictures
    # beside live ones. (The per-layout directories are cleared in run_capture,
    # once the app has said which layouts exist.)
    for old in out_dir.glob("*.png"):
        old.unlink()

    host = None
    try:
        if args.self_host:
            host = SelfHost(args)
            host.start()
            base_url, token, routes = host.base_url, host.token, host.routes
        else:
            base_url, token = args.base_url.rstrip("/"), args.token
            routes = discover_routes(base_url, token, args)

        print(f"CAPTURE {base_url} -> {out_dir}", flush=True)
        started = time.time()
        result = run_capture(base_url, token, routes, args, out_dir)

        # sort_keys + a trailing newline so the file is diffable and stable
        # whatever order the routes were captured in. These two are kept beside
        # the flat golden because they are what somebody actually reads when
        # debugging ONE route; the golden is what a reviewer reads.
        (out_dir / "requests.json").write_text(
            json.dumps(result["requests"], indent=2, sort_keys=True) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
        (out_dir / "focus.json").write_text(
            json.dumps(result["focus"], indent=2, sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")
        write_pixels(out_dir / "pixels.txt", result["pixels"])

        failures = result["failures"]
        if failures:
            print("\nFAILED captures:", file=sys.stderr)
            for line in failures:
                print("  " + line, file=sys.stderr)
            # Deliberately before any comparison: a golden written from a run
            # with a broken route would bake the breakage in, and a --check
            # against a partial run would report drift that is really an
            # absence. Fail on the capture, fix that, then talk about the diff.
            return 1

        lines = structure_lines(result, args, out_dir)
        (out_dir / "structure.txt").write_text("\n".join(lines) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
        print(f"\n{len(result['focus'])} captures in {time.time() - started:.0f}s "
              f"({len(lines)} structural facts), no console or page errors.")

        status = 0
        if args.update:
            write_golden(Path(args.golden), lines)
            print(f"UPDATE  {args.golden} — review the diff before committing it.")
        elif args.check:
            want = parse_golden(Path(args.golden).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
            got = parse_golden("\n".join(lines))
            changed, report = diff_golden(want, got, args.diff_lines)
            if changed:
                print(f"\nDRIFT   {changed} structural facts differ from {args.golden}:",
                      file=sys.stderr)
                for line in report:
                    print(line, file=sys.stderr)
                print("\n  If this change is intended: scripts/ui-baseline --self-host "
                      "--update, then commit the golden with it.", file=sys.stderr)
                status = 1
            else:
                print(f"CHECK   {len(want)} structural facts match {args.golden}.")

        if args.baseline:
            moved, added, gone = compare_pixels(result["pixels"], args.baseline)
            if moved or added or gone:
                print(f"\nPIXELS  vs {args.baseline}:")
                for key in moved:
                    print(f"  ~ {key} moved")
                for key in added:
                    print(f"  + {key} is new")
                for key in gone:
                    print(f"  - {key} is gone")
                # Not a gate: a moved pixel is the QUESTION "did you mean that?",
                # and only a human looking at the two PNGs can answer it. Making
                # it exit 1 would make every intended visual change a red run.
                print(f"  {len(moved)} moved, {len(added)} new, {len(gone)} gone — "
                      "open the PNGs in both directories to judge them.")
            else:
                print(f"\nPIXELS  identical to {args.baseline} "
                      f"({len(result['pixels'])} captures).")
        return status
    except Fail as e:
        print(f"error: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    finally:
        if host and not args.keep_server:
            host.stop()
        elif host:
            print(f"server left running at {host.base_url} (token {host.token})")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
